----------------------------------------------------------------------

1. What is m2tom3?

m2tom3 is a system to port Modula-2 programs to Modula-3. It consists
of two parts:

 - A conversion program which tries to create a Modula-3 source text
   with the same semantics as the Modula-2 input while retaining the
   original look and feel as good as possible.

 - A base library which tries to emulate the Modula-2 standard library
   using the Modula-3 standard library.

----------------------------------------------------------------------

2. Requirements

The system was developed and tested with Modula-3 3.3 on SPARC/SunOS
4.1.3. The conversion program should be quite platform-independent,
since no low-level operations are used. The base library, however,
depends in parts on an underlying Unix system and might be unusable on
other platforms. From version 2.03 on, m2tom3 and m2libs should work
with Solaris 2.4 and Modula-3 3.5.x as well.

----------------------------------------------------------------------

3. Installation

Unpack the distribution in an appropriate directory. To build the base
library, run m3build in the m2libs subdirectory. To build the
converter, run m3build in the m2tom3 subdirectory.

----------------------------------------------------------------------

4. The Converter

4.1. Overview

The m2tom3 program tries to create a Modula-3 source text with the
same semantics as its Modula-2 input. Although the languages look
quite similar in appearance, their semantics are pretty different in
some points. Some differences between them can be handled readily,
some are quite intricate, and some are practically impossible to
translate. For a detailed description of the differences we found
between Modula-2 and Modula-3 and if resp. how they are addressed by
the converter, see the appendix.

The program takes a few options, some of which are for development
purposes only. To see all the options, start the executable with
-h. The following options are useful for the final user:

Reporting:
-q      quiet: report no warnings, only errors
-v      verbose: report course of action to stdout
-F      File: include errors and warnings in the generated Modula-3
        source code as special comments

Paths:
-I path Include: add a search path for Modula-2 imports
-O path Output: set path for Modula-3 output

Transformation (see appendix):
-f      for: do not change FOR-loops into WHILE-loops
-s      string: do not change string literals
-r      make all call-by-value ARRAYs READONLY

Misc:
-h      help: show all options

All other parameters are supposed to be Modula-2 input files which
should be converted. For every <file>.def/<file>.mod input file, a
corresponding <file>.i3/<file>.m3 file is created.

Note 1: No recovery or extended type checking is performed. So, if you
feed the converter with source text which doesn't run through a
Modula-2 compiler, m2tom3 might produce wrong output or even crash.

Note 2: The quality of the Modula-3 output depends heavily on the fact
that m2tom3 can reach all declarations/definitions for the things you
use in your source text. Therefore, m2tom3 will analyse all definition
modules which are imported by the current unit. If m2tom3 can't find
them in the current directory, additional search paths should be set
up using the -I option. If a definition module cannot be found, m2tom3
is restricted in the transformations it can perform on the source
text.

Note 3: The converter can take an arbitrary number of source texts as
input. All these units are scanned and parsed into one big data
structure. This is advantageous because often used interfaces are
scanned and parsed only once and not over and over again for every
input unit which uses this interface. However, if the overall input
size passes a certain limit (depending on your machine), this data
structure gets so big that m2tom3's performance deteriorates
drastically.

4.2. Known Bugs and Quirks (also see Appendix A, Conversion Details)

Firstly and most important, m2tom3 is a Class A Resource Hog. The more
complex your input gets, the converter will run almost forever while
using an incredible amount of swap space, especially during the
identifier linkage phase. Right now, I don't know how to change
this. Sorry.

The converter makes some transformations on standard functions /
types. If you use their names for other purposes, m2tom3 may crash or
produce strange results. Particularly, check that you don't (re)define
the following identfiers: ADR, VAL, CHR, BYTE, SIZE, TSIZE, ODD, MIN,
MAX, HIGH.

Even if your Modula-2 compiler allows, don't reuse module names for
anything else. The identifier linker may get a headache.

If an enumeration type literal is also used for some other identifier,
m2tom3 gets mixed up.

Using anonymous types (i.e. types which are not explicitely named) for
FOR-loop index variables causes m2tom3 to use a NoType instead of
copying the type specification into the expression.

On several occasions, m2tom3 moves or copies identifiers from one
source text to another without bothering whether this identifier is
declared (imported) in the target unit.

In the declaration of variant records, the qualification of
enumeration type literals in the case label sequence is not removed if
there is more than one label.

* If you encounter problems not listed here, please let me know. *

4.3. A Tour through the Internals

m2tom3 was designed to allow transformations to be added or changed
easily. For those who want to tune the transformations to their needs,
this section gives an overview on how m2tom3 works. However, m2tom3
depends heavily on the underlying Modula-2 grammar which is given in
the appendix. If you want to convert source text which does not
correspond to this grammar, major changes to the program might be
inevitable.

The converter works in three phases:

 1. Analyzing
  1.1. The Scanner transforms the source text into a token stream.
  1.2. The Parser creates a concrete (!) syntax tree from the token
       stream. This tree is build on top of the token stream in the
       same data structure, i.e. the leafs of the syntax tree have
       pointers to the corresponding tokens in the stream.
  1.3. The IdentifierLinker adds edges to the syntax tree which
       connect applying and defining usages of identifiers.
  1.4. The ParameterLinker adds edges to the syntax tree which connect
       actual parameters in procedure calls to the types of the
       corresponding formal parameters in the procedure's
       definition/declaration.

  If you change anything in the Analyzer, the Generator may or may not
  break down completely.

 2. Generating
  The Generator runs through the syntax tree and looks for things to
  convert. Most transformations are handled in separate modules, you
  might take a look e.g. at the CHRGen module for a simple example.
  Most of the time, these modules traverse the syntax structure of the
  source to gather information and to insert or remove text at the
  right place. This is the reason for the generator being so dependent
  on the grammar: if you change the syntax tree structure, it is often
  very hard to determine which navigation operations have to be
  modified.

  The generation of new Modula-3 source text is handled only on token
  level, i.e. the syntax tree itself is not changed during the
  transformation. Furthermore, no tokens are actually removed from the
  token stream. Instead, each token knows to which language(s) it
  belongs. Initially, all tokens are assumed to be Modula-2 as well as
  Modula-3 tokens. During the transformation, new tokens are inserted
  which are Modula-3 tokens only, and other tokens are recognized not
  to be Modula-3 tokens.

 3. Saving
  Finally, the generated result is saved by traversing the token
  stream and writing all Modula-3 tokens to a file.

----------------------------------------------------------------------

5. The Library

After a source text has been converted using m2tom3, the resulting
Modula-3 program will generally not compile because the Modula-2
standard modules used by the program are not compatible with the
Modula-3 standard library. Therefore, the system provides some modules
which emulate this Modula-2 standard library. Note, however, that this
library is very restricted. First of all, it is based on the the
Modula-2 standard library from the Sun Modula-2 compiler, also
available in the EPC Modula-2 compiler. Even here, the emulation is
far from being complete. Furthermore, the library modules are written
to run on a Unix system. Last but not least, a Modula-3 program using
the Modula-2 library emulation instead of corresponding Modula-3
library functions will be much slower. So, if your program was written
for a compiler with a different standard library, you have to extend
the emulation library accordingly or modify the program to use the
Modula-3 library.

----------------------------------------------------------------------

6. Contributions

Since the m2tom3 system was developed in a project course, I won't be
able to put much work into it. But if you find errors in the program
or library, or if you make extensions or changes which might be useful
to others, please let me know. I will try to include them in future
releases.

----------------------------------------------------------------------

7. History & Credits

The historical roots of m2tom3 lie in a recursive-descendent Modula-2
parser written by Frank Hoefer in 1990. We used it to build several
Modula-2 applications like a pretty printer, a lint program, and a tag
file generator for Modula-2.

In 1991, I modified the pretty printer to produce Modula-3 instead of
Modula-2 output where possible. Of course, this approach could handle
only simple syntactical conversions. Later, I bootstrapped the system
by porting it to Modula-3. This was the first release of m2tom3, 1.00.

In the following years, only two minor bug fixes brought m2tom3 to
version 1.02.

In 1994, a project course was dedicated to a complete new
implementation of m2tom3. The course was supervised by Albert Zuendorf
and myself and attended by Roland Baumann, Gerhard Bosch, Kai Michael
Degner, Andre Eylmanns, Juergen Goebbels, Reiner Gombert, Joachim
Herrmann, Markus Jansen, Robert Kiehne, Marc Lindner, Reiner Nix,
Jean-Baptiste Petat, Alexander Poensgen, Jean-Luc Richard, Thomas
Stockheim, and Andreas Winter.

Version 2.01 is a bugfix release. Some errors and patches were
contributed by Rodney Bates (bates@salsv6.boeing.com).

Version 2.02 is another bugfix release. Most important, a bugfix in
2.01 introduced an even more serious bug. I hope this is solved now.

Version 2.03 contains minor bugfixes, support for the -r flag (makes
call-by-value ARRAY parameters READONLY), introduces Solaris 2.x
versions of OS-related interfaces, and adds M2MathLib and
M2ConvertReal modules to m2libs (credits to Roland Baumann).

See appendix C for a detailed description of the changes made since
version 2.00.

I'd like to thank all of the above for their efforts.

