Appendix A. Conversion Details

( 1) WITH Statement

    Modula-2:
      A WITH statement opens a new scope where the record fields
      of the designated record are visible. The designator is
      calculated once (important to side effects).

    Changes to Modula-3:
      The record fields aren't visible any more. The designator part
      of the statement doesn't match Modula-3 syntax.

    Handling:
      A Modula-3 WITH statement is introduced which declares an
      identifier. This identifier is assigned the expression from
      the Modula-2 WITH statement. Occurences of record components
      within the statement are qualified by the new identifier.

( 2) Grammar Rule of Block

    Modula-2:
      Block ::= {Declaration} ['BEGIN' StatementSequence] END

    Changes to Modula-3:
      Block ::= {Declaration} BEGIN StatementSequence END

      The keyword BEGIN can't be ommited.

    Handling:
      The BEGIN keyword is inserted if not present.

( 3) Local Modules

    Modula-2:
      Every declaration part may contain local modules.

    Changes to Modula-3:
      Local modules aren't supported.

    Handling:
      None.

( 4) Empty Procedure Signature

    Modula-2:
      A procedure specification, definition, declaration or call may
      omit parentheses when the signature is empty. This problem
      includes also procedure variables and procedure assignments.

    Changes to Modula-3:
      At least parentheses must be written.

    Handling:
      Missing parentheses are inserted.

( 5) String and CHAR Literals

    Modula-2:
      1. The type of string literals is ARRAY [0..n] OF CHAR.
      2. String literals may be enclosed by single or double quotes.
      3. 'a', "a" may be a string or CHAR literal.

    Changes to Modula-3:
      1. The type of string literal is TEXT.
      2. String literals are enclosed by double quotes.
      3. CHAR literals are enclosed by single quotes.

    Handling:
      1. String literals are converted to ARRAY OF CHARs using an
         array constructor. We have four different cases:
         - The literal occurs in a constant declaration.
           Then, an array with an additional zero character at the
           end is generated. The size of the array is generated to
           exactly contain all characters.
         - The literal occurs as an actual parameter, and the type
           of the corresponding formal parameter is an ARRAY OF CHAR.
           This is handled as above.
         - The literal occurs as an actual parameter, and the type
           of the corresponding formal parameter is some
           X = ARRAY [a..b] OF CHAR.
           Here, an X constructor is generated with {\000,  ..} at
           the end. Note that this may fail if the literal contains
           exactly as many characters as X can hold.
         - The literal occurs in the RHS of an assignment.
           Then, a constructor without zero character is generated on
           the RHS and a SUBARRAY expression is generated for the LHS.
           The zero character is appended if the RHS variable is big
           enough.
         Note that this conversion can be switched off with the -s
         option.
         Note also that the cases in which the array occurs as an
         actual parameter won't be handled if the procedure is called
         via a procedure variable.
      2., 3. This is handled by the Scanner using a heuristic. This
             fails frequently when a one-character ARRAY OF CHAR is
             found.

( 6) Enumeration Types

    Modula-2:
      1. Enumeration elements in an enumeration type declaration
         are enclosed in round brackets.
      2. Enumeration literals are implicitly visible.

    Changes to Modula-3:
      1. Enumeration elements in an enumeration type declaration
         are enclosed in braces.
      2. Enumeration literals are qualified by their enumeration
         type name.

    Handling:
      1. The brackets are replaced.
      2. Handled by qualifying the enumeration literals.
         This fails, of course, if the enumeration type is
         anonymous.

( 7) Variant Records

    Modula-2:
      A record definition may contain a variant part.

    Changes to Modula-3:
      Variant records aren't supported.

    Handling:
      The variant record is 'flattened', i.e. all variant field
      will become regular fields.
      Since the variant record may have been used for casting,
      the applying occurences of variant record fields are marked
      by warnings. There is no way for the converter to handle
      this case in general.

( 8) Operator Precedence

    Modula-2:
      Operator NOT has priority over all other operators.

    Changes to Modula-3:
      Operator NOT has lower priority than most other operators
      (may be there are more changes).

    Handling:
      Handled by inserting parentheses around SimpleExpr, Term,
      NotFactor if these items are not atomic.

( 9) Typetransfer Using Type Names

    Modula-2:
      A type name may be used like a function to transfer the type of
      an expression:

        VAR
          s: S;
          t: T;
        BEGIN
          s := S(t);
        END

    Changes to Modula-3:
      The unsafe type transfer operator LOOPHOLE should be used:

          s := LOOPHOLE(t, S);

    Handling:
      Works for predefined types only.

(10) Predefined (Standard) Datatypes

    Modula-2:
      Some standard or widely common datatypes are predefined like:
      BITSET, BYTE, WORD, SHORTCARD, LONGCARD, SHORTINT, LONGINT,
      LONGREAL.

    Changes to Modula-3:
      These types don't exist.

    Handling:
      Some of the types are defined in the m2libs SYSTEM module,
      and an IMPORT is generated for them. CARDINAL is converted
      into Word.T to retain unsigned int values.

(11) CCALL Statements

    Modula-2:
      CCALL statements are used to call C procedures directly out of
      the program. The called procedure is passed to CCALL as a string
      in double quotes, an arbitrary number of parameters are passed
      to the called C procedure by CCALL. CCALL has either no return
      value or type integer depending on the called C procedure.

          CCALL("bcopy", from, to, len);

    Changes to Modula-3:
      CCALL doesn't exist.

    Handling:
      The C procedures are assumed to be implemented in the Modula-3
      module CCALL. The implementation may use external pragma.
      The Modula-2 CCALL statement is replaced by importing the module
      CCALL and calling the procedure determined by first argument of
      the old statement, like in

          CCALL.bcopy(from, to, len);

(12) NEW and DISPOSE Pseudo Procedures

    Modula-2:
      NEW and DISPOSE are pseudo procedures.

          NEW(t);
          DISPOSE(t);

      They will be expanded by the compiler to:

        BEGIN
          ALLOCATE(t, TSIZE(T));
          DEALLOCATE(t, TSIZE(T));
        END

      Where t is assumed to be of type POINTER TO T. Procedures named
      ALLOCATE and DEALLOCATE are assumed to be imported from an
      arbitrary module.

    Changes to Modula-3:
      Syntax and semantic of NEW and DISPOSE change.
      The procedures are internally realized statements.

    Handling:
      The pseudo procedures will be expanded to:

          ALLOCATE(LOOPHOLE(t, ADDRESS), BYTESIZE(T));
          DEALLOCATE(LOOPHOLE(t, ADDRESS), BYTESIZE(T));

(13) Opaque Types

    Modula-2:
      An opaque type is declared in an interface by omitting its
      realization. Originally, opaque types are restricted to pointer
      types (T) but some implementations allow also other types (S).
      Opaque types are realized by using a normal type declaration in
      the appropriate implementation.

      DEFINITION MODULE M;
        TYPE T;
        TYPE S;
      END M.

      IMPLEMENTATION MODULE M;
        TYPE T = POINTER TO TRep;
        TYPE S = SRep;
      END M.

    Changes to Modula-3:
      Because of the generalization of the concept, the syntax of the
      definition changes. Case (S) is not allowed. The realization uses
      the REVEAL keyword:

      INTERFACE M;
        TYPE T <: ADDRESS;
        TYPE S = SRep;
      END M.

      MODULE M;
        REVEAL T = UNTRACED BRANDED REF TRep;
      END M.

    Handling:
      (a) regular opaque types (T) are declared as subtypes of ADDRESS
          and revealed with the old internal representation.

      (b) other opaque types (S) are completely declared in the
          interface. They are deleted from the implementation.

(14) Standard Type ADDRESS

    Modula-2:
      The standard type ADDRESS is compatible to all pointer types:

        PROCEDURE P (VAR adr: ADDRESS);
        VAR t: POINTER TO T;
        BEGIN
          P(t);
        END;

    Changes to Modula-3:
      The compatibility rules with respect to ADDRESS are more
      restricted: ADDRESS is only a subtype of UNTRACED REF T. As a
      result of the modified compability rules, it isn't necessary to
      transform assignments but you can't pass an arbitrary pointer to
      a variable argument of type ADDRESS. You should use the LOOPHOLE
      operation:

        PROCEDURE P (VAR adr: ADDRESS);
        VAR t: POINTER TO T;
        BEGIN
          P(LOOPHOLE(t, ADDRESS));
        END;

    Handling:
      Handled by casting the type using LOOPHOLE.

(15) Standard Types BYTE / WORD

    Modula-2:
      (a) The standard types WORD and BYTE denote types which are
          assignment compatible to all other types of the same size.

      (b) In particular, formal parameters of type ARRAY OF BYTE,
          ARRAY OF WORD are compatible to nearly arbitrary types.

    Changes to Modula-3:
      These types and the related exceptional compatibility rules
      don't exist.

    Handling:
      BYTE and WORD are regularly declared integer types in the
      m2libs module SYSTEM.
      For the ARRAY OF BYTE type, LOOPHOLEs are introduced at the
      caller's side. An especially nasty case is the following:

      PROCEDURE P(a: ARRAY OF INTEGER; b: ARRAY OF BYTE);
      BEGIN
         P(a, a);
      END P;

      Here, an open ARRAY OF AnythingButBYTE is passed to an
      ARRAY OF BYTE. This is handled as follows:
         <Create tmp: ARRAY OF BYTE with the byte size of a>
         <Copy a into tmp bytewise>
         <Call the procedure with tmp instead of a>
         <Copy tmp's value back to a>

      Note that all of these conversions won't work if the procedure
      is called via a procedure variable.

(16) Unit Identification

    Modula-2:
      Modula-2 knows MODULE, IMPLEMENTATION MODULE, and DEFINITION
      MODULE to determine the module type.

    Changes to Modula-3:
      Modula-3 has MODULE and INTERFACE instead.

    Handling:
      The keywords are changed. For the MODULE, an additional
      EXPORTS Main is generated. Furthermore, all units are marked
      UNSAFE since it is very unlikely that any output from m2tom3
      is safe (in the Modula-3 sense).

(17) ADR Operator

    Modula-2:
      The ADR operator may be used to determine the address of a
      variable.

    Changes to Modula-3:
      Using Modula-3's ADR on an array will return a pointer to a
      structure which contains the array shape followed by its
      contents, not a pointer to the contents itself.

    Handling:
      ADR(x) is replaced by ADR(x[FIRST(x)]) if x is an array. This
      may fail if x is multi-dimensional.

(18) VAL Operator

    Modula-2:
      Modula-2's VAL operator allows conversion between discrete types.

    Changes to Modula-3:
      The order of the VAL operands is reversed.

    Handling:
      The operand order is reversed.

(19) CHR Operator

    Modula-2:
      Modula-2's CHR operator allows conversion from CARDINAL to CHAR.

    Changes to Modula-3:
      The CHR operator is not supported.

    Handling:
      The CHR operator is replaced by an appropriate VAL operator.

(20) FOR Loops

    Modula-2:
      Modula-2 has the FOR loop construct
        FOR <x> := <a> TO <b> BY <s> DO <Block> END;

    Changes to Modula-3:
      The FOR loop in Modula-3 looks quite the same, but here the
      index variable is implicitly declared and read-only.

    Handling:
      FOR loops are converted into WHILE loops to retain the same
      semantics. You can turn this off with the -f switch.

(21) Pointers

    Modula-2:
      Pointers are declared with POINTER TO <Type>.

    Changes to Modula-3:
      Pointers are declared with REF. Modula-3 distinguishes between
      traced and untraced pointer types.

    Handling:
      POINTER TO is translated into UNTRACED BRANDED REF.

(22) Procedure Types

    Modula-2:
      When declaring procedure types, Modula-2 takes only the
      corresponding formal parameter types.

    Changes to Modula-3:
      Signatures are just as in any procedure definition.

    Handling:
      Dummy parameter names are inserted, commas are replaced by
      semicolons.

(23) SET Operations

    Modula-2:
      Uses INCL and EXCL to add or remove elements from a set.

    Changes to Modula-3:
      Uses + and - with set constructors for this.

    Handling:
      None.

(24) Language Interfacing

    Modula-2:
      Uses a DEFINITION FOR C MODULE (in Sun's M2C) or something
      similar for setting up interfaces to foreign-language objects.
      
    Changes to Modula-3:
      Uses the <* EXTERNAL *> pragma for this.

    Handling:
      None.

(25) Empty Statements

    Modula-2:
      The Modula-2 grammar allows empty statements, introduced e.g.
      by successive semicolons or semicolons before an END, UNTIL,
      ELSE etc.

    Changes to Modula-3:
      Modula-3 doesn't allow this.

    Handling:
      None.

(26) CASE Statement

    Modula-2:
      1. Labels and statements are separated by colons.
      2. Cases may be empty, resulting in one or more bars with
         no following label.

    Changes to Modula-3:
      1. Labels and statements are separated by =>.
      2. Empty cases are not allowed.

    Handling:
      1. Separators are replaced.
      2. Bars with no subsequent case are removed.

----------------------------------------------------------------------

Appendix B. The Modula-2 Grammar

CompilationUnit         ::= DefinitionModule | ProgramModule

DefinitionModule        ::= `DEFINITION` ForIdentOpt `MODULE` PublicScope
PublicScope             ::= DIdent `;` DefinitionScope `END` AIdent `.`
ForIdentOpt             ::= e | `FOR` Ident
DefinitionScope         ::= ImportList ExportList DefinitionList

ProgramModule           ::= ImplementationOpt `MODULE` PrivateScope
PrivateScope            ::= DIdent `;` ProgramScope AIdent `.`
ImplementationOpt       ::= e | `IMPLEMENTATION`
ProgramScope            ::= ImportList Block


DefinitionList          ::= e | Definition DefinitionList
Definition              ::= ConstDefinition | TypeDefinition | VarDefinition | ProcedureDefinition

ConstDefinition         ::= `CONST` ConstList
ConstList               ::= e | DIdent `=` ConstExpr `;` ConstList

TypeDefinition          ::= `TYPE` TypeOptList
TypeOptList             ::= e | DIdent TypeOpt `;` TypeOptList
TypeOpt                 ::= e | `=` Type

VarDefinition           ::= `VAR` VarList
VarList                 ::= e | DIdent DIdentList `:` Type `;` VarList


ProcedureDefinition     ::= `PROCEDURE` DIdent FormalScope `;`
FormalScope             ::= FormalParametersOpt


ImportList              ::= e | Import ImportList
Import                  ::= QualifiedImport | UnqualifiedImport

QualifiedImport         ::= `IMPORT` QEntryScope
QEntryScope             ::= DAIdent DAIdentList `;`

UnqualifiedImport       ::= `FROM` UEntryScope `IMPORT` ImportScope`;`
UEntryScope             ::= DAIdent
ImportScope             ::= DAIdent DAIdentList


ExportList              ::= e | Export ExportList
ExportOpt               ::= e | Export
Export                  ::= `EXPORT` QualifiedOpt ExportScope `;`

QualifiedOpt            ::= e | `QUALIFIED`
ExportScope             ::= DAIdent DAIdentList


Block                   ::= DeclarationList BlockStatementsOpt `END`
BlockStatementsOpt      ::= e | `BEGIN` StatementOptSeq


DeclarationList         ::= e | Declaration DeclarationList
Declaration             ::= ConstDefinition | TypeDeclaration | VarDefinition |
                            ModuleDeclaration | ProcedureDeclaration

TypeDeclaration         ::= `TYPE` TypeDeclarationList
TypeDeclarationList     ::= e | DIdent `=` Type `;` TypeDeclarationList


ModuleDeclaration       ::= `MODULE` DIdent PriorityOpt `;` ModuleScope AIdent `;`
PriorityOpt             ::= e | `[` ConstExpr `]`
ModuleScope             ::= ImportList ExportOpt Block


[1]
ProcedureDeclaration    ::= `PROCEDURE` DIdent OuterScope
OuterScope              ::= FormalParametersOpt  `;` ProcedureTail
ProcedureTail           ::= `FORWARD` | ProcedureBody
ProcedureBody           ::= InnerScope ExtraScope
InnerScope              ::= Block
ExtraScope              ::= AIdent `;`


FormalParametersOpt     ::= e | FormalParameters
FormalParameters        ::= `(` ParameterSectionSeqOpt `)` ReturnParameterOpt

ReturnParameterOpt      ::= e | `:` QualIdent


ParameterSectionSeqOpt  ::= e | ParameterSection ParameterSectionList
ParameterSectionList    ::= e | `;` ParameterSection ParameterSectionList
ParameterSection        ::= VarOpt DIdent DIdentList `:` FormalType

VarOpt                  ::= e | `VAR`


Type                    ::= ProcedureType | PointerType | SetType | ArrayType | RecordType | SimpleType


ProcedureType           ::= `PROCEDURE` FormalSignatureOpt


FormalSignatureOpt      ::= e | FormalSignature
FormalSignature         ::= `(` SignatureSectionSeqOpt `)` ReturnSignatureOpt

ReturnSignatureOpt      ::= e | `:` SimpleType

SignatureSectionSeqOpt  ::= e | SignatureSection SignatureSectionList
SignatureSectionList    ::= e | `,` SignatureSection SignatureSectionList
SignatureSection        ::= VarOpt FormalType


FormalType              ::= ArrayOpt QualIdent
ArrayOpt                ::= e | `ARRAY` `OF`


PointerType             ::= `POINTER` `TO` Type


SetType                 ::= `SET` `OF` Type


ArrayType               ::= `ARRAY` SimpleType SimpleTypeList `OF` Type
SimpleTypeList          ::= e | `,` SimpleType SimpleTypeList


RecordType              ::= `RECORD` RecordScope `END`
RecordScope             ::= FieldOptSeq


FieldOptSeq             ::= FieldOpt FieldOptList
FieldOptList            ::= e | `;` FieldOpt FieldOptList
FieldOpt                ::= e | Field


Field                   ::= SimpleField | CaseField

SimpleField             ::= DIdent DIdentList `:` Type
CaseField               ::= `CASE` DIdentOpt `:` QualIdent `OF` VariantOpt VariantOptList ElseFieldOpt `END`
ElseFieldOpt            ::= e | `ELSE` FieldOptSeq


VariantOptList          ::= e | `|` VariantOpt VariantOptList
VariantOpt              ::= e | CaseLabelSeq `:` FieldOptSeq


SimpleType              ::= Enumeration | Subrange | QualIdentOrSubrange

Enumeration             ::= `(` EnumerationScope `)`
EnumerationScope        ::= DIdent DIdentList

SubrangeOpt             ::= e | Subrange
Subrange                ::= `[` ConstExpr `..` ConstExpr `]`

QualIdentOrSubrange     ::= QualIdent SubrangeOpt



StatementOptSeq         ::= StatementOpt StatementOptList
StatementOptList        ::= e | `;` StatementOpt StatementOptList
StatementOpt            ::= e | Statement
Statement               ::= AssignmentOrCallStatement | IfStatement | CaseStatement | WhileStatement |
                            RepeatStatement | LoopStatement | ForStatement | WithStatement |
                            ExitStatement | ReturnStatement

AssignmentOrCallStatement ::= Designator AssignmentOrCall
AssignmentOrCall        ::= Assignment | ActualParametersOpt
Assignment              ::= `:=` Expr

ExitStatement           ::= `EXIT`
ReturnStatement         ::= `RETURN` ExprOpt


IfStatement             ::= `IF` Expr `THEN` StatementOptSeq ElseIfList ElseOpt `END`
ElseIfList              ::= e | `ELSIF` Expr `THEN` StatementOptSeq ElseIfList
ElseOpt                 ::= e | `ELSE` StatementOptSeq


CaseStatement           ::= `CASE` Expr `OF` CaseOptSeq ElseOpt `END`


CaseOptSeq              ::= CaseOpt CaseOptList
CaseOptList             ::= e | `|` CaseOpt CaseOptList
CaseOpt                 ::= e | Case
Case                    ::= CaseLabelSeq `:` StatementOptSeq


CaseLabelSeq            ::= CaseLabel CaseLabelList
CaseLabelList           ::= e | `,` CaseLabel CaseLabelList
CaseLabel               ::= ConstExpr CaseRangeOpt

CaseRangeOpt            ::= e | `..` ConstExpr


WhileStatement          ::= `WHILE` Expr `DO` StatementOptSeq `END`


RepeatStatement         ::= `REPEAT` StatementOptSeq `UNTIL` Expr


ForStatement            ::= `FOR` AIdent `:=` Expr `TO` Expr ByExprOpt `DO` StatementOptSeq `END`
ByExprOpt               ::= e | `BY` Expr


LoopStatement           ::= `LOOP` StatementOptSeq `END`


WithStatement           ::= `WITH` Designator `DO` WithScope `END`
WithScope               ::= StatementOptSeq


Designator              ::= AIdent DesignatorTailList
DesignatorTailList      ::= e | DesignatorTail DesignatorTailList
DesignatorTail          ::= DesignatorScope | Reference | SubscriptionScope

DesignatorScope         ::= `.` AIdent

Reference               ::= `^`

SubscriptionScope       ::= `[` Expr ExprList `]`


ActualParametersOpt     ::= e | ActualParameters
ActualParameters        ::= `(` ExprSeqOpt `)`


ExprSeqOpt              ::= e | Expr ExprList
ExprList                ::= e | `,` Expr ExprList
ExprOpt                 ::= e | Expr
Expr                    ::= SimpleExpr RelationOpt

RelationOpt             ::= e | Relation SimpleExpr
Relation                ::= `=` | `#` | `<` | `<=` | `>` | `>=` | `IN`


SimpleExpr              ::= UnaryAddOpt Term AddTermList
UnaryAddOpt             ::= e | UnaryAdd
UnaryAdd                ::= `+` | `-`
AddTermList             ::= e | BinaryAdd Term AddTermList
BinaryAdd               ::= `+` | `-` | `OR`


Term                    ::= Factor MulFactorList
MulFactorList           ::= e | Mul Factor MulFactorList
Mul                     ::= `*` | `/` | `DIV` | `MOD` | `REM` | `AND` | `&`


Factor                  ::= Integer | Long | String | Char | Real |
                            FactorExpr | NotFactor | QualSetOrCall | Set

FactorExpr              ::= `(` Expr `)`

NotFactor               ::= `NOT` Factor

QualSetOrCall           ::= QualIdent SetOrCall
SetOrCall               ::= Set | Call
Call                    ::= DesignatorTailList ActualParametersOpt


Set                     ::= `{` ElementSeqOpt `}`


ElementSeqOpt           ::= e | Element ElementList
ElementList             ::= e | `,` Element ElementList
Element                 ::= Expr ElementRangeOpt

ElementRangeOpt         ::= e | `..` Expr


ConstExprSeqOpt         ::= e | ConstExpr ConstExprList
ConstExprList           ::= e | `,` ConstExpr ConstExprList
ConstExpr               ::= SimpleConstExpr SimpleRelationOpt

SimpleRelationOpt       ::= e | Relation SimpleConstExpr


SimpleConstExpr         ::= UnaryAddOpt ConstTerm AddSimpleTermList
AddSimpleTermList       ::= e | BinaryAdd ConstTerm AddSimpleTermList


ConstTerm               ::= ConstFactor MulConstFactorList
MulConstFactorList      ::= e | Mul ConstFactor MulConstFactorList


ConstFactor             ::= Integer | Long | String | Char | Real |
                            ConstFactorExpr | NotConstFactor | QualConstSetOrCall | ConstSet

ConstFactorExpr         ::= `(` ConstExpr `)`

NotConstFactor          ::= `NOT` ConstFactor

QualConstSetOrCall      ::= QualIdent ConstSetOrCall
ConstSetOrCall          ::= ConstSet | ConstActualParametersOpt

ConstActualParametersOpt::= e | `(` ConstExprSeqOpt `)`


ConstSet                ::= `{` ConstElementSeqOpt `}`


ConstElementSeqOpt      ::= e | ConstElement ConstElementList
ConstElementList        ::= e | `,` ConstElement ConstElementList
ConstElement            ::= ConstExpr ConstRangeOpt

ConstRangeOpt           ::= e | `..` ConstExpr


DIdentOpt               ::= e | DIdent
DIdentList              ::= e | `,` DIdent DIdentList

DAIdentList             ::= e | `,` DAIdent DAIdentList

QualIdent               ::= AIdent QualificationScope
QualificationScope      ::= e | `.` AIdent QualificationScope


[1] (ProcedureDeclaration)
  The identifier seems to be declaring if the procedure is a
  forward-declaration or if it is a complete declaration without a
  forward. Otherwise, when used as complete declaration with forward,
  it seems to be an applying identifier.
  This grammar handles the identifier only as declaring because this
  information is only used when linking the identifiers, and this code
  part doesn't ignore that the procedure is declared twice.

